response:
 First, we calculate the side length of square A using its perimeter. The perimeter of a square is given by \(4 \times \text{side length}\). Let the side length of square A be \(s_A\). Then, we have: \[
4s_A = 24 \implies s_A = \frac{24}{4} = 6 \text{ cm}
\] The area of square A is \((s_A)^2 = 6^2 = 36 \text{ cm}^2\). Since the area of square B is one-fourth the area of square A, the area of square B is \(\frac{36}{4} = 9 \text{ cm}^2\). The side length of square B, \(s_B\), can be found by taking the square root of the area: \[
s_B = \sqrt{9} = 3 \text{ cm}
\] The perimeter of square B is \(4 \times s_B = 4 \times 3 = 12 \text{ cm}\). Therefore, the perimeter of square B is \(\boxed{12}\).</think> <answer> 12</answer>

